# renki. Design

The launcher library. The root readme is the shipping description of what it
does and how a tool is written against it, and it is compiled as a doctest
through a private `Readme` item so the usage block cannot go stale. This
document says what the readme leaves to the code and what the configuration
adds.

## What was already here

A `Tool` descriptor, a pin resolved to build attempts, a keyed build cache
under the cache root and a registry with a self-update marker under the state
root, both roots from `renki-dirs`. `PinSource` records in the registry which
form a repository's pin arrived in, so the collector can tell a build a
repository re-pinned away from one it still wants. The `Backend` contract and
the `Cargo` backend materialise an engine; `materialise_once` is what a
launcher calls for its own build.

## Configuration

The launcher reads its two configuration files through `renki-manifest`,
which finds each file's layers, answers its predicates and holds the TOML
store, and keeps for itself what is a launcher's: the `--cfg` flag, the
variables handed to the engine, and the `config` verbs. Each of the two files
is a stack of layers, `<config_file>` and every `<name>.<layer>.<ext>` the
manifest rules find for it.

The reader resolves every declared setting once, in the design's order: a
`--cfg key=value` flag, the tool's `<SHORT>_CFG_<KEY>` variable, the
repository's `<config_file>` stack where the setting's scope allows it, the
person's `<config root>/<config_file>` stack, then the default; inside a
stack, the highest layer that holds the key, with a list appended across the
layers as the manifest rules say. A key the schema
does not know is refused by name from any of those places, and a value the
kind refuses is refused with its source named. The engine receives the result
as `<SHORT>_CFG_<KEY>` per setting plus `<SHORT>_CONFIG_FILE` naming the
person's file, the way it receives `--dir`, and never has two answers to choose
from. `<SHORT>_CONFIG` is the config directory's override, `renki-dirs`'s, and
is a different variable. A tool with no settings gets none of this and keeps
its arguments as they were, `--cfg` included, since that flag is then the
engine's to read.

`widget config` joins `widget locate` as a query the launcher answers without
the engine, since the engine is what is being configured and may not build:
`path`, `schema`, `get <key>`, `set <key> <value>` and `edit`. `get` prints
the source beside the value, and for a value from a file its layer,
`repo:local` for `<name>.local.<ext>`; `path` lists every layer read, in its
order. The output is `key=value` lines like `locate`.
`set` and `edit` both create the person's config directory when it is not
there yet, and `set` puts a key where a reader would look for it: a top-level
key at the end of the top level, which is above the first table header when
the file opens with one, and a dotted key at the end of its table, or in a new
table at the end where the file has none. `set` and `edit` write the base
file; `--layer <word>` on either writes `<name>.<word>.<ext>` instead,
creating it where it is not there yet.

## A tool's own commands

`locate` and `config` are the crate's, fixed in name and in output. A tool
that has a question of its own the engine cannot be asked declares it on the
descriptor: `Tool::commands` is a table of `Command`, each a name, a sentence
and a function, and the launcher tries the table where it tries `config`,
after its own flags are off the arguments and before a root is required. The
case this exists for is `homma workspace`, which spawns a workspace where the
cwd is not in one, so there is no config above the cwd, no root, no pin and
no engine to build.

The function gets an `Invocation`, read through accessors and built by nobody
outside the crate: the tool, the cwd, the root where one was found, the
settings resolved from the two files, and what followed the name with the
launcher's own flags taken out wherever they sat, since `--dir`, `--engine`
and `--cfg` are the launcher's before and after the name alike. It answers
`Result<(), String>` the way a hook does, and a refusal is printed and exits
nonzero the way `config`'s is. The settings arrive as the resolved rows the
engine environment is built from, one per declared key with the canonical
text and the source, so a command reads a value through the same kind an
engine would and a list walks through `TextItems`. Nothing about the row
changes for this beyond its visibility.

A command is not a hook. It runs only when named, sees no pin and cannot reach
the engine, since the engine may not be buildable where it runs. What the
descriptor check refuses: an empty name, since the loop would match every
bare argument; a name that is `locate`'s subcommand, or `config` on a tool
with settings, since the crate's query is tried first and the tool's would
never run; a name that is the directory flag, the engine flag, or `--cfg` on a
tool with settings, since those come off the arguments before the table is
looked at and the command would vanish, be refused as a flag with nothing
after it, or be swallowed as a value; and two commands of one name. All of it
is const and settles at build time with the rest of `Tool::defect`.

The alternative was the consumer matching the name in its own `main` ahead of
`renki::run`, with no change here. The table wins because the command gets
the settings resolved by the same reader the engine environment comes from,
and because the collision check above can only be made where the crate's own
names are known, which a match in `main` runs before.

## A hosted tool's descriptor

`extension::Descriptor` is a parsed `tool.toml` and refuses unknown fields, so
what a host wants its tools to say is added here. Three fields join it.
`who`, a list of words naming whom the tool is for, which renki keeps and
interprets none of; a host matches them against its own roles. `moment`, one
line saying when the tool is reached for, kept and shown. And `settings`,
rows of `key`, `kind`, `default`, `scope` and `doc` in the shape of
`renki-config`'s `Setting`, each turned into a `renki_config::Declared` over
the descriptor's text by `Declared::named`, and checked by
`Declared::defect` when the descriptor is parsed, so a bad row refuses the
descriptor the way a bad `run` does.

The kinds a descriptor may name are the ones whose parsers need nothing but
the text: `bool`, `int`, `text`, `path` and `list`. A `choice` is refused by
name, since its options are a type's and a descriptor has none.

Where a hosted tool's values come from is the host's: it resolves the rows
over the documents it chooses through `renki_config::resolve`, and
`extension::command` hands the tool the result as `<NAME>_CFG_<KEY>`, the
descriptor's `name` as the short name, the way a launcher hands its engine
`<SHORT>_CFG_<KEY>`.

## The retried install

A failed `cargo install` is tried once more after a short pause, since the
failure that prompted it was a registry deleted under the build and the second
attempt succeeded alone. Before the first attempt the launcher checks that
`cargo` is on `PATH` and refuses naming that rather than failing inside
cargo's output. Two attempts, then the existing failure message with the retry
recorded in it. The engine's build in `materialise` and the launcher's own
reinstall in `selfupdate` go through the same `install_with_retry`, after the
same check.

## One build per key

The cache key is a hash of the whole compilation input: the engine's url, the
resolved revision and the toolchain identity from `rustc_fingerprint`, which
`compute_key` folds in. A change in any of the three is a different key, and a
key's build lands once at `<cache>/builds/<key>/bin/<engine>`, shared by every
repository pinned to it.

`cargo install --root` locks its install root only for the final move of the
finished binary, so the compile itself would run once per launcher that
resolved the key. `ensure_built` therefore holds an exclusive lock on
`<cache>/builds/<key>.lock`, a `BuildLock`, across the whole build. A launcher
that finds the lock taken says it is waiting, blocks until the holder lets go,
and then looks for the binary again before it builds anything, so the second
launcher on a key returns the first one's engine. The lock is the operating
system's, so it goes when its holder does and a killed build leaves nothing to
detect as stale. It sits beside the install root rather than in it, so a
collection pass evicting the build does not remove what a waiting launcher
holds.

Each key installs through a cargo target directory of its own, at
`<cache>/builds/<key>/target`, so a killed build resumes. Two keys never
install through one target directory, which is why the key's lock is the only
lock renki takes: a launcher waits only on a build of its own key.

What stays in the cache is the binary and nothing else. The intermediates go
where the consuming repository's cargo puts its own: `build_dir` reads cargo's
configuration from the repository's root as a `cargo build` there would, the
variables first, then each `.cargo/config` or `.cargo/config.toml` from the
root upward, the extensionless one where both are there, then `$CARGO_HOME`'s,
and takes `build.build-dir`, or else the target directory. A file that does not
parse is an error, as it is for cargo. `{workspace-root}` in the value is the
repository's root, the workspace it was written for; a value with
`{workspace-path-hash}` names no build directory, since the hash cargo would
compute for the install is of the installed package's checkout and not of the
repository. `cargo install` reads no project configuration itself, so the plan
carries the answer as `CargoPlan::build_dir` and the install gets it as
`CARGO_BUILD_BUILD_DIR`. The key's target directory then holds only the final
artifacts, and the install shares every unit that comes out the same as the
repository's own; the install does not read the repository's `[patch]`, its
flags or its profile overrides, so a unit those change is compiled again. The
install is always given `CARGO_BUILD_BUILD_DIR`, never left to cargo: where no
build directory is read for the repository, or the one read names
`{workspace-path-hash}`, it is the install's own target directory, so neither
`$CARGO_HOME`'s configuration nor an inherited variable can send the
intermediates to a directory per revision. Either way a finished install
removes the key's target, so no second copy of the binary stays behind; a
killed build keeps it and resumes. A key whose binary is already there and
whose target an earlier launcher left has the target removed by the next run,
under the key's lock. The `--engine` scratch build follows the same rule. The
launcher's own reinstall does too, and since every reinstall on the machine
shares one target, it holds a lock beside it for the whole reinstall, so one
reinstall never removes the directory another compiles into. A launcher that
finds that lock held does not wait for it: it skips its update for this run
and goes on to the command, and the next check comes round within the hour.

A build no repository resolves to any more is removed the moment the registry
records the run that left it, not on the daily pass: a repository re-resolving
to a new key leaves its old key with no consumer, and keeping it would hold one
engine per revision the pin moved through that day. So there is one binary per
pin in use. A key still resolved by some repository stays until the retention
window, on the daily pass. A key whose build lock is held stays with its row.
Loading the registry, recording the run, removing and saving happen under one
lock on a file beside the registry, so two launchers never save over what the
other recorded or remove a build the other has just recorded.

The collector tries the key's lock before it removes a key, without waiting. A
key whose lock is held is being built, so it is left in place with its row, and
the next pass looks at it again. A key it can lock is not being built, and the
lock is held while the directory goes, so a launcher that starts on the key
meanwhile waits for the removal and then builds it afresh.

The toolchain in the key and the toolchain that builds are the one that answers
in the repository's root, so a `rust-toolchain.toml` there decides both and one
revision is not keyed twice. `CargoPlan::dir` is that root, and cargo runs in
it, for the pinned build and for the `--engine` scratch build alike. Neither
takes a toolchain from whoever started the launcher: `in_selected_toolchain`
removes `CALLER_TOOLCHAIN_VARS` (`RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN`, which rustup's proxy hands
down from a cargo pinned to another toolchain, and its siblings) from both the
key's `rustc -vV` and the build's `cargo install`, so a launcher started under
such a cargo and one started from a plain shell key and build one pin once. The
fingerprint on the `Cargo` backend itself reads rustc in the launcher's own
directory, because the contract it implements takes no plan; the launcher's key
does not use it and reads the repository's root directly. For the same reason
the cache root is made absolute before the build runs: the build is in the
repository's root and cargo resolves `--root` and `--target-dir` there, while
the lookups resolve in the launcher's own directory, and a relative
`<SHORT>_CACHE` would otherwise name two places.

The one interleaving the lock does not cover is a collection pass evicting a
build between the build and the exec. The run re-materialises a bounded number
of times, which is handled beside the lock and not by it.

## A new revision builds from its own sources

An install from git compiles each revision from that revision's sources, in the
shared build directory as everywhere else. Cargo alone would not: `cargo
install --git` loads its checkout under `$CARGO_HOME` as a path workspace, so a
package's unit is hashed by its path inside the checkout, the same for every
revision, and cargo never looks at the files under `$CARGO_HOME` to see whether
they changed. A unit another revision left in the build directory is then
fresh, and the install hands back the old revision's binary under the new
key. A repository's own build of the same packages lands on the same units. A
version from the registry is keyed by the version and is not open to it.

So every install reports its units as cargo's JSON messages
(`--message-format json-render-diagnostics`, the diagnostics still rendered to
the operator), and `path_units` reads each path package and whether its unit
came back fresh. Where an attempt that names `--git` took a fresh path unit,
`forget_units` removes the fingerprints of every path package that install
reported from the build directory's release profile, and the attempt runs once
more, which compiles them from this revision. The registry's units stay
shared. An install that compiled every path package itself runs once. A key
built again for a revision it had before runs twice, and is correct either way;
a fresh path unit is the only signal rather than a record of which revision
last built a package, since a record cannot answer for a build directory it
never saw.

## No cargo inside a build script

A launcher reached from a build script runs no cargo at all. Cargo holds the
build directory's lock for the whole of a build, build scripts included, and
the install compiles in the repository's build directory, so a `cargo install`
started there waits on the build that started it and never returns. The
program of a `cargo run` and a `cargo test` binary run with that lock free,
and they are not refused. `inside_a_build_script` tells the three apart by
`BUILD_SCRIPT_VARS`: `CARGO`, `OUT_DIR`, `TARGET` and `HOST` all set, which
cargo does for a build script alone, the last two being the ones the other two
runs lack. The sketch `202611191450_cargo-holds-its-build-lock` measured both.

An engine already built for the key runs as it is, so the common case, a tool
called from a build script long after its engine was built, is unchanged. A
key with no engine is refused by `ensure_built` before it takes or waits on the
key's lock, since a launcher outside the build holding that lock can itself be
waiting on the build directory's. `refuse_inside_a_build_script` writes the
refusal: the run is a build script's, so the engine for this pin is not built
here, and running the tool once from a shell in the repository's root builds
it. The failure is a `BuildError::NotStarted`, which forgets no resolution.
`Cargo::build_with` refuses the same way before its first attempt, which is
what covers the `--engine` scratch build. The self-update is skipped for the
run, as under the opt-out variable, since a missed check comes round within
the hour and a reinstall there would wait the same way.

## Placement is decided once

A backend either places its material itself, holding its own exclusion over
the destination, or leaves that to the caller, which builds into a scratch
directory and renames the finished tree into place. `Backend::places_itself`
says which, because it is a fact about the backend and not about the caller:
`Cargo` places itself, since `cargo install --root` takes its own lock and
moves the binary in. `place` is the one body that decides, and both routes in
go through it. `materialise_once` names the backend at compile time, and
`locate` finds it in a registry, so a precondition added to `place` reaches
both and the two cannot drift into separate dispatches that merely agree.

The repository's lint `the-placement-branch-exists-once` holds this. It reads
the parse of `extension.rs`, and it fails when the branch on `places_itself`
appears more than once there, or once outside `place`. It replaces a test that
read the file's text, since a check on source text runs on every commit as a
lint and not only when the suite does.

## Asking a remote for a branch head

A branch pin's head and the launcher's own head are both read with
`git ls-remote`, through `ls_remote_head`, and it answers within five seconds
or not at all. The five seconds are one deadline over the whole ask, reading
how ssh is configured included, so the listing gets what the read left. Past
the deadline the child is killed and the call returns an error naming the
deadline, which both callers already take as a remote that
could not be reached: a branch pin falls back to its last resolution, unless
that was forgotten because its build was refused, which "A refused build forgets
its resolution" below describes, and the self-update leaves the installed
launcher alone. Waiting for git to give up
instead meant a network dropping packets stalled every launch for as long as
git took, and the fallback that exists for that network only started after.

git does not prompt on the terminal: `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT` is `0` and stdin is
closed. A credential helper is told not to open a window, with
`credential.interactive` set to `never` for the one call, which Git Credential
Manager honours and a helper that ignores the setting does not. A credential
already stored is still used. ssh runs in `BatchMode` only where nothing has
said how ssh runs: not
`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`, not `GIT_SSH`, and not `core.sshCommand`, read in that order
and only as far as the first that answers, so the configuration is read only
when the environment says nothing. Setting the first over either of the others
would override it, since git ranks it above both, and the key or agent somebody
chose there would be lost. Where ssh is configured it is left alone, so it may
still ask for a passphrase on the terminal. The deadline holds over the answer
and not over the prompt: at five seconds git is killed and the call returns its
error, while the ssh or the helper git started runs on, so a prompt or a window
can still be up after the launcher has moved on.

The child's output goes to two scratch files rather than pipes, removed once
read, so a child writing more than a pipe holds cannot stall on a launcher
that is only waiting for it to exit. The answer is what the child had written
when it exited: something it leaves running with its output still open, an ssh
control master say, is not waited for at all. Only the child is killed at the
deadline; what it started finishes on its own.

## A refused build forgets its resolution

A branch pin asks the remote for its head on every launch, one `git ls-remote`
inside its deadline, so a moved branch is built on the launch after it moved,
and a head that did not move keys the build already in the cache and makes no
cargo call. The recorded resolution is only the offline fallback. A build of the
resolved revision that failed would otherwise leave that fallback naming a
revision that does not build. `resolved_and_built` pairs the resolution with
its build and returns a `Built`: the resolution, the toolchain, the key and the
binary. When the build was refused, `forget_resolution` removes the recorded
resolution of that url and branch, and the next launch asks the remote for the
tip.

Only a refusal forgets. A `BuildError` tells the two failures apart by its type
and not by its message. `Refused` is a build that started and came to nothing,
cargo having failed every attempt or having succeeded without leaving the
binary. `NotStarted` is one that could not start: cargo missing from the path,
the key's lock not taken, the directory not made. The second says nothing about
the revision and keeps the resolution, so that an operator without cargo is told
so and not that the remote is out of reach.

A refusal is not always a broken revision. Cargo fails the same way when the
network drops under a dependency's download, and from here that looks like one.
Forgetting then costs one `git ls-remote` on the next run, inside its deadline,
and loses no fallback, because the revision that was refused has no build in the
cache for the fallback to find.

The forget names the revision that was refused and removes the record only while
it still names that revision. A launcher that resolved the branch again while
this one built has recorded a tip of its own, and that record stays. A build
refused on the rebuild after an eviction forgets in the same way as the first
build, with no exception: the key holds the toolchain, so a moved toolchain is a
new key and not a refusal of the old one.

Other branches of the same remote keep their resolutions, a pin that names no
branch has none to forget, and a build that worked keeps its own. The
`Backend::materialise` contract keeps its text error, since it is public and
waits for the stack's string type. `Cargo` turns a `BuildError` into its message
there, and the launcher's own build reaches the typed function through `place`,
which is generic over its error so that `materialise_once` still answers with
text.
